{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Then Moses sang.</b> When he saw the miracle, the thought arose in his heart to sing a song. So too ״then Joshua spoke״ (Joshua 10), and so too ״and a house he shall make for Pharaoh's daughter״ (I Kings 7) — he thought in his heart to make it for her; so too here, יָשִׁיר — his heart told him to sing, and so he did: ״and they spoke, saying: I will sing to YHWH.״ And so with Joshua — when he saw the miracle, his heart told him to speak, and so he did: ״and he said before the eyes of Israel״ (Joshua 10). And so the Song of the Well, which opens with ״Then Israel sang״ — after it he explained ״Spring up, O well, sing to it״ (Numbers 21). ״Then Solomon built a high place״ (I Kings 11) — the Sages of Israel explained that he sought to build but did not build; this teaches us that the yod is said for the thought. This is to settle its plain sense. But its midrash — our Rabbis of blessed memory said: ״From here is an allusion to the revival of the dead from the Torah.״ And so in all of them, except that of Solomon, which they explained as: he sought to build but did not build. And one should not say to settle this word like other things written in future tense that are immediate, such as ״Thus did Job do״ (Job 1), ״at the mouth of YHWH they encamp״ (Numbers 9), ״and sometimes the cloud would be״ (ibid.) — for these are ongoing matters that fit both future and past tense; but this one, which was only for a single moment, I cannot settle by that approach." "<b>For He is highly exalted.</b> As its Targum renders. (Another interpretation: the repetition comes to say that He did a thing impossible for flesh and blood to do — when one fights another and overpowers him, he knocks him off the horse, but here the horse and his rider He hurled into the sea; and anything impossible to do by another falls under the term גֵאוּת, as in ״for He has done triumphantly״ (Isaiah 12). And so the whole song you will find doubled — עָזִּי וְזִמְרָת יָהּ / וַיְהִי לִי לִישׁוּעָה / יְהוָה אִישׁ מִלְחָמָה / יְהוָה שְׁמוֹ, and so all of them.) Another interpretation — <b>for He is highly exalted</b>: beyond all songs and all that I praise in Him, there is yet more — and not like the measure of a king of flesh and blood, whom they praise but there is nothing more in him." "<b>The horse and his rider.</b> Both are bound to each other, and the waters lift them up and carry them down to the depths, and they do not separate (Mekhilta)." "<b>Hurled.</b> Threw, as in ״and they hurled him into the pit of the fiery furnace״ (Daniel 3). And its aggadic midrash: one verse says רָמָה, and one verse says יָרָה — this teaches that they would rise to the height and sink to the abyss, as in ״who cast its cornerstone״ (Job 38) — from above downward."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:52:24.121714819Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}